  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 259 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nForecast for next week looks to remain largely the same from day\nto day under weak pressure gradients aloft with a few chances\nfor some scattered precipitation, but otherwise a cool start to\nthe weak with a slow and steady warming trend through the\nremainder of. Upper level pattern will consist of weak ridging\ntrying to build in on Monday, but this will be overtaken by a\nweak low lifting up from California and slowly progressing\nacross the region through the end of the forecast period. At the\nsurface, Monday will see our forecast region in a post-frontal\nenvironment, with a weak boundary then sitting across the WY/NE\nborder area and then lifting east-northeast as a warm front into\nthe later days of the week. Under this pattern Monday will be\nthe coolest day of the forecast period as widespread highs\ngenerally sit in the 60's to right around 70 in the warmest\nspots. Increasing warmth then expected as warmer air from the\nsouthwest moves across the region, but don't expect a crazy\nwarmup as we increase only 1-3 degrees each day, keeping the\nregion mostly in the 70's through the week until Thursday when a\nfew more 80's may finally begin popping up. A brief blip of\nmoisture may produce increased chances of showers and storms on\nTuesday as the California low lifts up, but without a stout\nforcing mechanism don't expect much outside of general showers\nand thunderstorms and coverage isolated to scattered at best.\nOtherwise, a few isolated showers or storms possible each day as\nwell with nothing impactful expected.",
